{"author":{"id":"2eecc7d99b502c158aacb0868dca9358610edc02724d21fe49b0196402d3364e"},"ops":[{"type":3,"timestamp":1785102254,"nonce":"gHraYH7Z7ZFPlIc3vynXB7NgQ18=","message":"FIXED as ADR-0061 **s8**: a Workspace Snapshot now has two coordinates, and only\none of them is an address.\n\n|  | covers | is an address? | answers |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| **snapshot root** | names, targets, modes, **mtimes** | yes — `trees/\u003chash\u003e` | where are these exact bytes? |\n| **content identity** | names, targets, modes | **no** | is this the same content? |\n\nThe identity is the same merkle fold with every mtime dropped and each sub-tree\nnamed by *its* identity. Folded up for free during `ingest` (one extra SHA-256\nper directory, zero round-trips — nothing is stored under it), carried on the Pod\nas `scarab.io/workspace-identity` in the same patch as the root, recorded beside\nthe root as Attempt evidence (migration `0040`), and compared by admission's\ninput signature. The root stays the one true address: materialize, GC's mark\nwalk, browse and `prune_tree` are all unchanged.\n\nWhich of the three options, and why not the other two:\n\n1. **Two hashes** — this one, with the ticket's \"it doubles the tree vocabulary\"\n   priced down by making the second digest *not* an address. It is a label on\n   evidence: you cannot fetch it, so there is no second namespace, no second GC\n   problem, and no ambiguity about which is \"the root\".\n2. **Normalise mtimes on ingest** — rejected as **unsafe**, not merely as\n   throwing away s7. mtime-based build tools are wrong in exactly one direction:\n   a timestamp that is too *old* makes them skip a rebuild they needed. Constant\n   mtimes make every inherited source look ancient, so a **Cache** (ADR-0007)\n   restored beside the workspace with real timestamps looks *newer* than the\n   sources it was built from, and make/cargo skip. Wrong output, not slow output.\n3. **Compare blob sets instead of roots** — right instinct, and s8 is its cheap\n   form. Comparing sets means walking two trees at admission time; one derived\n   digest answers the same question in a string compare, computed where the tree\n   is already in hand.\n\nAlso considered: **carry mtimes forward from a baseline** (on ingest, reuse the\nprevious snapshot's mtime for any path whose content is unchanged). This is the\nonly scheme that keeps ONE digest and stays safe, and it is elegant — a\nreproducible root *and* every timestamp truthful about when content last\nchanged. It loses to ADR-0061's own retention model: the baseline is a snapshot,\nsnapshots expire (warm by space, cold by TTL), and when it is gone the mtimes\nrevert to wall clock and the root moves again. A determinism guarantee that\ndecays silently is the failure being fixed.\n\nAnd the fourth option the ticket did not list — **drop mtime from the preimage\nand carry it as unhashed entry metadata** — was rejected for a sharper reason\nthan s7 gave. s7 objected that content-identical trees would collide and\n`put_if_absent` makes the first-stored win, so timestamps become\nnon-deterministic. The counter is that same-identity means same content, so\nserving either one's timestamps cannot mislead a tool comparing files *within*\nthe tree. That counter fails, because CI tools compare against state *outside*\nthe tree: a path whose content went X → Y → X (a revert, a flaky generator, two\nbranches sharing a store) is served the week-old X timestamp, and a Cache built\nfrom Y yesterday looks newer — the same wrong-output class as option 2. It also\nbreaks the CAS's defining property: the key would stop determining its content,\nso a tree could not be verified against its address at all (blobs are), and\n`materialize` would stop being a pure function of the root.\n\n\n- `crates/scarab-storage-s3/tests/hashing.rs::a_re_ingest_at_a_new_wall_clock_keeps_the_content_identity`\n  — the ticket's shape through the real CAS: byte-identical content ingested ten\n  seconds apart. Roots MUST differ (else s7's fidelity regressed); identities\n  MUST NOT; both roots still materialise on their own.\n- `…::the_content_identity_moves_for_every_change_that_is_not_a_timestamp` — the\n  other direction, including a **nested** file's bytes, which only reaches the\n  root identity because sub-trees are named by identity rather than by hash.\n- `…::the_content_identity_matches_its_independently_derived_preimage` — identity\n  preimages and digests pinned against literals recomputed in Python\n  (`json.dumps(separators=(\",\",\":\"))` + `hashlib.sha256`); the same script\n  re-derives the existing tree-hash pins as a control, and both match.\n- `crates/scarab-db-postgres/tests/restart.rs::a_rerun_whose_root_churns_but_whose_content_does_not_still_skips`\n  — engine grain, real Postgres: a producer whose root moves per attempt and\n  whose content does not; the dependent is still skipped, then cascades when the\n  identity changes. Verified failing on the old behaviour (dependent re-ran).\n- `crates/scarab-executor-k8s/tests/cluster.rs::workspace_flows_from_a_to_b_through_the_cas`\n  — the narrowed assertion is restored and widened: it now asserts BOTH that the\n  two roots differ (s7) and that the two identities match (ADR-0027).\n\n\n`restart.rs::rerun_skips_unchanged_descendant_then_cascades_when_output_changes`\nwas **not** a test that could not fail — it can fail, and it does fail if the\nadmission rule breaks. But it drives a `FakeExecutor` whose output hash is a\n*string the test handed it*, so \"the re-run produced the same output\" is true by\nconstruction and it is structurally blind to a storage-layer determinism failure.\n`fidelity.rs` and `hashing.rs`'s round-trip both re-ingest a fixed clock. So the\ncomposed case — real CAS, same bytes, new clock — had no test at all. It has two\nnow, at both grains, and the blind spot is documented at the old test's site.\n\n\nThe **tree preimage is untouched**, so no stored snapshot is orphaned and\n`hashing.rs`'s existing literals are unchanged. Migration `0040` adds two\nNULLable columns with no backfill; a pre-s8 row compares by\n`COALESCE(output_identity, output_snapshot)`, i.e. by its root, which cascades\nwhere it might have skipped — wasteful, never wrong. And a snapshot recording no\nmtimes has an identity *equal* to its root, so for those rows the fallback is\nexact rather than merely conservative.\n\n\nThe live kube tier (`SCARAB_TEST_KUBE`) was not run — the restored cluster\nassertion is the one piece of this that is still unexercised, and it is the tier\nthat found the bug in the first place.","files":null}]}